{{$NEXT}}

  - Async Forgejo client on IO::Async and Net::Async::HTTP, a thin transport
    layer over WWW::Forgejo (request building, auth, JSON and errors);
    released in lockstep with WWW::Forgejo 0.001
  - Future-based verbs get/post/put/patch/delete/check taking the WWW::Forgejo
    options params, upload (multipart) and raw; delete accepts a body
  - check() takes its verdict from the sync client, so both clients answer
    2xx with yes, 404 with no and every other status with an error
  - Non-blocking auto-pagination via X-Total-Count (page_size, max_pages),
    including the search envelope and the Actions runs envelope
    (workflow_runs, total from the body's total_count when there is no
    X-Total-Count header), returning the same shape as WWW::Forgejo
  - Response headers pass through to WWW::Forgejo::HTTPResponse; body bytes
    and charset are handled like WWW::Forgejo::LWPIO
  - New "timeout" configure key (default 30 s, as WWW::Forgejo::LWPIO) so a
    stalled server fails the Future instead of leaving it pending forever
  - New "http" configure key to inject a preconfigured transport
  - configure(url/token) now takes effect for later requests
  - url and base_url delegate to WWW::Forgejo (no more doubled /api/v1 or
    trailing slash mismatch)
  - https URL without a loadable IO::Async::SSL fails the Future at once
    with a message naming the module
  - API errors arrive as failed Futures ("Forgejo API error: ..."), on
    follow-up pages too
